  "mood": "Dheerasankarabharanam has a mellifluous, smooth, melodious, and gently majestic character. It creates a feeling of serenity and balance, while also supporting devotional depth and dignified grandeur. The raga can feel expansive, bright, and reassuring, like a calm yet regal presence. Its even spacing and major-scale clarity make it suitable for both relaxed, laid-back expression and stately, uplifting elaboration, often leaving the listener with a sense of poise, sweetness, and fullness.",
  "description": "Dhīraśankarābharaṇaṃ, commonly known as Śankarābharaṇaṃ, is the 29th melakarta rāga in the 72 melakarta system of Carnatic music. It is the 5th rāga in the 5th chakra, Bāṇa, with mnemonic name Bāṇa-Ma and mnemonic phrase 'sa ri ga ma pa da ni sa'. Its scale is S R2 G3 M1 P D2 N3 S / S N3 D2 P M1 G3 R2 S, using shadjam, chatushruti rishabham, antara gandharam, shuddha madhyamam, panchamam, chatushruti dhaivatam, and kakali nishadam. As a melakarta, it is by definition sampurna in both ascent and descent. It is the shuddha madhyamam equivalent of the 65th melakarta Kalyani. The raga is highly celebrated and, because it accommodates many gamakas, is glorified as 'Sarva Gamaka Māṇika Rakti Rāgaṃ'. Its nature is described as mellifluous and smooth, with large scope for compositions and for melodious yet laid-back majestic presentation. Owing to the even spacing of its swaras, many janya ragas arise from it, and it is one of the melakarta ragas with a very high number of derived ragas. It is also one of the major concert ragas with strong scope for elaboration, experimentation, and exploration of phrases. In Hindustani music its scale corresponds to Bilaval, and in Western music it corresponds to the major scale or Ionian mode. Its notes, under graha bhedam, yield five other major melakarta ragas: Kalyani, Hanumatodi, Natabhairavi, Kharaharapriya, and Harikambhoji. A seventh graha-bhedam result contains both madhyamams and no panchamam, so it is not considered a valid melakarta, though it may still be musically useful in theory. In simplified form, these related scales can be played on the white keys of a piano or keyboard. The raga is also one of the scales whose name remains the same in both the asampurna and current melakarta systems.",
  "time_of_day": "",
  "gamaka_usage": "This raga is explicitly noted for supporting many gamakas and is honored as 'Sarva Gamaka Māṇika Rakti Rāgaṃ', indicating rich ornamentational potential. Its gamaka treatment is broad and flexible rather than restricted, allowing expansive melodic elaboration. In practice, G3 and N3 are important carriers of kampita and nuanced oscillation, while D2 also admits graceful oscillation and curved descent into P. R2 is often approached with light oscillation or as part of fluid R-G movement rather than as a stark isolated note. M1 and P generally function as comparatively steady nyasa or resting points, giving structural firmness amid ornamented movement. Jaru phrases across adjacent swaras are natural because of the even scalar spacing, and phrases such as S R G M, G R S, P D N S, and S N D P can be rendered either plainly or with measured slides depending on context. The raga tolerates plain-note treatment unusually well—one reason it is central to pedagogy and also maps neatly to the major scale—but in concert music its full rakti emerges through layered gamakas, especially on G3, N3, D2, and in the linking of R2-G3-M1. Ornamentation should enhance sweetness, majesty, and melodic fullness rather than produce a harsh or vivadi effect. Compared with more phrase-restricted ragas, Sankarabharanam allows both scalar clarity and gamaka-rich manodharma, making it suitable for expansive alapana, tanam, neraval, and kalpana swara.",
